    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d594">

                <pb facs="0760" n="652" sameAs="frus:frus1891/pg652"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Douglass</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1891-02-13"><hi rendition="#italic">February 13,
                        1891</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 97.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received your No. 120, of the 26th
                    ultimo, replying to my No. 89, of the 9th ultimo, and reporting the result of
                    your conferences with Mr. Firmin on the inconveniences caused to American
                    commerce by the Haitian law requiring the detention of vessels in Haitian ports
                    until the import duties on their cargoes are paid by the consignees.</p>
                <p>You will continue to keep the matter in view. Messrs. Brett, Son &amp; Co., of
                    New York, have been informed of your efforts to remedy the evil complained
                    of.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">James G. Blaine</hi>.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
